{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01R56hjLJp1XwWbFDEuvVR6s","type":"tool_result","content":"   190→**Step 0.1: Create a feature branch**\n   191→\n   192→All commands in this plan assume the working directory is the `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave` repository root (the directory containing `package.json`, `server/`, `scripts/`, etc.). If running from a different directory, `cd` to the repo root before starting. Relative paths in scripts (e.g., `import.meta.dir` + `\"..\"`) and `bun run` commands all depend on this.\n   193→\n   194→```bash\n   195→git checkout develop\n   196→git pull origin develop\n   197→git checkout -b hrishi/monorepo-migration\n   198→```\n   199→\n   200→**Step 0.2: Verify clean state and establish baseline**\n   201→\n   202→This step is critical — do not skip it. The baseline established here is the only way to distinguish pre-existing failures from regressions introduced by the migration.\n   203→\n   204→First, verify the working directory is clean:\n   205→```bash\n   206→git status  # must be clean — no uncommitted or untracked changes\n   207→```\n   208→\n   209→If `git status` shows existing modifications (e.g., from a prior attempt or another agent's partial work), decide how to proceed: either `git checkout -- . && git clean -fd` to reset to a clean `develop` state, or carefully assess what has already been done, verify it matches the plan, and continue from that point. The plan is written assuming a clean start. If continuing from partial work, re-run Phase 6 verification at the end to confirm correctness.\n   210→\n   211→Then run the baseline checks and record results:\n   212→```bash\n   213→bun run test:unit\n   214→bun run lint\n   215→bun run tc\n   216→```\n   217→\n   218→Record the baseline results before making any migration changes. This establishes what passes and what fails before the migration, so the implementer can distinguish pre-existing issues from regressions.\n   219→\n   220→**Known pre-existing issues (as of the source snapshot used for planning):**\n   221→\n   222→1. **`bun run tc` (typecheck) fails.** The `tsconfig.json` specifies `\"types\": [\"bun-types\"]`, but the `bun-types` package is stored in `node_modules/.bun/bun-types@<version>/node_modules/bun-types/` rather than directly at `node_modules/bun-types/`. The TypeScript compiler cannot find it via normal module resolution. The newer `@types/bun` package is present and would work, but `tsconfig.json` still references the older package name. This is unrelated to the monorepo migration and should not block it. The implementer should note the failure, confirm it also occurs on a clean `develop` checkout without migration changes, and proceed.\n   223→\n   224→2. **`bun run test:unit` has 9 pre-existing failures.** These are Codex binary locator tests that fail with \"Codex binary not found in node_modules. Ensure @openai/codex-sdk is installed.\" The expected baseline is approximately 1512 pass, 4 skip, 9 fail. These failures are environment-specific (missing Codex SDK binary) and unrelated to the migration. The implementer should confirm the same 9 tests fail on a clean checkout and proceed.\n   225→\n   226→If the implementer encounters additional failures beyond these known ones after making migration changes, those should be investigated as potential regressions. A quick way to check is `git stash && bun run test:unit && git stash pop` to compare before/after.\n   227→\n   228→### Phase 1: Bring docs into the repo\n   229→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f84b7c0b-88c6-4182-bc07-ac4fb387923f","uuid":"6bd49dcc-8430-4ad0-9f01-f9143abca0b2","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/philip/projects/hw-budget-tests/plan-gen-execution/agentRoot/plan/merged/plan.md","content":"**Step 0.1: Create a feature branch**\n\nAll commands in this plan assume the working directory is the `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave` repository root (the directory containing `package.json`, `server/`, `scripts/`, etc.). If running from a different directory, `cd` to the repo root before starting. Relative paths in scripts (e.g., `import.meta.dir` + `\"..\"`) and `bun run` commands all depend on this.\n\n```bash\ngit checkout develop\ngit pull origin develop\ngit checkout -b hrishi/monorepo-migration\n```\n\n**Step 0.2: Verify clean state and establish baseline**\n\nThis step is critical — do not skip it. The baseline established here is the only way to distinguish pre-existing failures from regressions introduced by the migration.\n\nFirst, verify the working directory is clean:\n```bash\ngit status  # must be clean — no uncommitted or untracked changes\n```\n\nIf `git status` shows existing modifications (e.g., from a prior attempt or another agent's partial work), decide how to proceed: either `git checkout -- . && git clean -fd` to reset to a clean `develop` state, or carefully assess what has already been done, verify it matches the plan, and continue from that point. The plan is written assuming a clean start. If continuing from partial work, re-run Phase 6 verification at the end to confirm correctness.\n\nThen run the baseline checks and record results:\n```bash\nbun run test:unit\nbun run lint\nbun run tc\n```\n\nRecord the baseline results before making any migration changes. This establishes what passes and what fails before the migration, so the implementer can distinguish pre-existing issues from regressions.\n\n**Known pre-existing issues (as of the source snapshot used for planning):**\n\n1. **`bun run tc` (typecheck) fails.** The `tsconfig.json` specifies `\"types\": [\"bun-types\"]`, but the `bun-types` package is stored in `node_modules/.bun/bun-types@<version>/node_modules/bun-types/` rather than directly at `node_modules/bun-types/`. The TypeScript compiler cannot find it via normal module resolution. The newer `@types/bun` package is present and would work, but `tsconfig.json` still references the older package name. This is unrelated to the monorepo migration and should not block it. The implementer should note the failure, confirm it also occurs on a clean `develop` checkout without migration changes, and proceed.\n\n2. **`bun run test:unit` has 9 pre-existing failures.** These are Codex binary locator tests that fail with \"Codex binary not found in node_modules. Ensure @openai/codex-sdk is installed.\" The expected baseline is approximately 1512 pass, 4 skip, 9 fail. These failures are environment-specific (missing Codex SDK binary) and unrelated to the migration. The implementer should confirm the same 9 tests fail on a clean checkout and proceed.\n\nIf the implementer encounters additional failures beyond these known ones after making migration changes, those should be investigated as potential regressions. A quick way to check is `git stash && bun run test:unit && git stash pop` to compare before/after.\n\n### Phase 1: Bring docs into the repo\n","numLines":40,"startLine":190,"totalLines":946}},"timestamp":"2026-03-05T13:50:48.259Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SMuBLZZUdLm1L8osvKfLYU","type":"tool_result","content":"   617→\n   618→### Phase 6: Verify everything works\n   619→\n   620→**Step 6.1: Run the full test suite**\n   621→```bash\n   622→bun run lint\n   623→bun run tc\n   624→bun run test:unit\n   625→bun test tests/integration\n   626→```\n   627→\n   628→Note on `test:integration`: the npm script `\"test:integration\": \"bun tests/integration\"` is a pre-existing quirk. Bun interprets `bun tests/integration` as \"run the file at path `tests/integration`\" (which is a directory, not a file), rather than \"run tests in that directory.\" The correct invocation for running integration tests is `bun test tests/integration` (with `test` as a subcommand). Use the direct `bun test tests/integration` invocation shown above rather than `bun run test:integration`. See Section g, pre-existing issue #3 for details.\n   629→\n   630→None of these should be affected by the migration, provided the `tsconfig.json` exclude for `docs/**/*` was added in Step 2.1. Without that exclude, `bun run tc` will fail here. The runtime code is unchanged, test imports use relative paths from `tests/` to `server/`, and Biome's `includes` whitelist only covers runtime files (not `docs/` or `scripts/`).\n   631→\n   632→**Expected pre-existing failures (see Phase 0, Step 0.2 for details):** `bun run tc` will fail due to the `bun-types` resolution issue, and `bun run test:unit` will show approximately 9 pre-existing failures related to Codex binary resolution. These are not regressions — they match the baseline established before migration. The implementer should verify the same tests fail (no new failures introduced) and proceed. If new failures appear beyond the baseline, investigate as potential regressions by stashing changes and re-running.\n   633→\n   634→**Step 6.2: Run schema generation**\n   635→```bash\n   636→bun run generate-schemas\n   637→```\n   638→Verify output exists in both `schemas/` and `docs/public/schemas/`.\n   639→\n   640→Note: the unified lockfile may resolve slightly different dependency versions than the previous root-only lockfile (e.g., `typescript` may resolve to a newer patch version). This can cause minor cosmetic changes to schema output (a few lines differing in `schemas/hankweave.schema.json`). In implementation testing, 4 lines changed in the schema file after the migration's `bun install` step. This is a one-time artifact of the dependency resolution change and is expected — include the schema diff in the migration commit. Future `generate-schemas` runs will be stable.\n   641→\n   642→**Step 6.3: Run the runtime build**\n   643→```bash\n   644→bun run build\n   645→```\n   646→Verify `dist/index.js` is created. The build script uses `import.meta.dir` relative paths and `\"./server/index.ts\"` as entry point -- these are all unaffected by the workspace addition.\n   647→\n   648→**Step 6.4: Verify docs build**\n   649→```bash\n   650→cd docs && bun run build && cd ..\n   651→```\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f84b7c0b-88c6-4182-bc07-ac4fb387923f","uuid":"842bcb1e-f9ce-4a1b-a368-f237177abb70","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/philip/projects/hw-budget-tests/plan-gen-execution/agentRoot/plan/merged/plan.md","content":"\n### Phase 6: Verify everything works\n\n**Step 6.1: Run the full test suite**\n```bash\nbun run lint\nbun run tc\nbun run test:unit\nbun test tests/integration\n```\n\nNote on `test:integration`: the npm script `\"test:integration\": \"bun tests/integration\"` is a pre-existing quirk. Bun interprets `bun tests/integration` as \"run the file at path `tests/integration`\" (which is a directory, not a file), rather than \"run tests in that directory.\" The correct invocation for running integration tests is `bun test tests/integration` (with `test` as a subcommand). Use the direct `bun test tests/integration` invocation shown above rather than `bun run test:integration`. See Section g, pre-existing issue #3 for details.\n\nNone of these should be affected by the migration, provided the `tsconfig.json` exclude for `docs/**/*` was added in Step 2.1. Without that exclude, `bun run tc` will fail here. The runtime code is unchanged, test imports use relative paths from `tests/` to `server/`, and Biome's `includes` whitelist only covers runtime files (not `docs/` or `scripts/`).\n\n**Expected pre-existing failures (see Phase 0, Step 0.2 for details):** `bun run tc` will fail due to the `bun-types` resolution issue, and `bun run test:unit` will show approximately 9 pre-existing failures related to Codex binary resolution. These are not regressions — they match the baseline established before migration. The implementer should verify the same tests fail (no new failures introduced) and proceed. If new failures appear beyond the baseline, investigate as potential regressions by stashing changes and re-running.\n\n**Step 6.2: Run schema generation**\n```bash\nbun run generate-schemas\n```\nVerify output exists in both `schemas/` and `docs/public/schemas/`.\n\nNote: the unified lockfile may resolve slightly different dependency versions than the previous root-only lockfile (e.g., `typescript` may resolve to a newer patch version). This can cause minor cosmetic changes to schema output (a few lines differing in `schemas/hankweave.schema.json`). In implementation testing, 4 lines changed in the schema file after the migration's `bun install` step. This is a one-time artifact of the dependency resolution change and is expected — include the schema diff in the migration commit. Future `generate-schemas` runs will be stable.\n\n**Step 6.3: Run the runtime build**\n```bash\nbun run build\n```\nVerify `dist/index.js` is created. The build script uses `import.meta.dir` relative paths and `\"./server/index.ts\"` as entry point -- these are all unaffected by the workspace addition.\n\n**Step 6.4: Verify docs build**\n```bash\ncd docs && bun run build && cd ..\n```","numLines":35,"startLine":617,"totalLines":946}},"timestamp":"2026-03-05T13:50:48.274Z"}
